# SRG-APP-000116-AU-000270  -  CAT III
# The Central Log Server must be configured to use internal system clocks to
# generate time stamps for log records.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations, then kept
# by hand. Edit this file to change how this requirement is assessed here;
# it is read at run time. Regenerating refreshes the benchmark text in place
# and leaves the criteria, the determination and any verification block as
# they are.

id:              SRG-APP-000116-AU-000270
group:           V-206457
severity:        low
cat:             CAT III
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      derived
cci:             CCI-000159
control_rev5:    AU-8

contribution:    Splunk Enterprise stamps each event either from the timestamp its own
                 timestamp processor recognizes in the event text or from the clock of the
                 host running splunkd, and props.conf is the only place that choice is
                 configurable, so the application's share is to leave timestamp assignment
                 with splunkd rather than accepting a time handed up by the input layer.

governs:         props.conf - How incoming data is recognized, parsed and timestamped.

check:           props.conf [<source type>] DATETIME_CONFIG not_equals NONE

external:        Accuracy and synchronization of the host clock that splunkd reads - the
                 operating system time service (NTP or chrony), which no Splunk setting
                 reaches
external:        Time zone of the host operating system, used when an event carries no zone
                 in its raw text and props.conf TZ is unset (props.conf.spec:580-592)

citation:        SRG-APP-000116-AU-000270 (V-206457) check text; props.conf.spec:502-519,
                 580-592
finding:         props.conf sets DATETIME_CONFIG = NONE for one or more source types, so
                 the event time is carried up from the input layer instead of being
                 assigned by splunkd from the host system clock.
